<p>A small kitchen can work very well when the cabinet layout is planned around how you actually cook. These ideas focus on using every usable centimetre without making the room feel crowded.</p>

<h2>Start with the layout</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Single-wall:</strong> all cabinets along one wall. Simple and space-saving; keep the sink, hob and fridge in a sensible working order.</li>
<li><strong>Galley (two parallel runs):</strong> efficient for cooking, but leave enough aisle width to open doors and drawers comfortably.</li>
<li><strong>L-shaped:</strong> uses a corner and keeps the work triangle short. Corner storage deserves special attention.</li>
</ul>
<p>Measure carefully before you choose; our <a href="/calculating-kitchen-cabinet-space/">measuring guide</a> shows how.</p>

<h2>Storage ideas that suit compact kitchens</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Go vertical:</strong> use cabinets up to the ceiling and keep the top shelf for things you rarely need.</li>
<li><strong>Drawers instead of doors for base cabinets:</strong> you see and reach everything without crouching.</li>
<li><strong>Pull-out organisers:</strong> narrow pull-outs beside the hob can hold oils, spices and utensils.</li>
<li><strong>Corner solutions:</strong> corner carousels or pull-out corner units make otherwise dead space reachable.</li>
<li><strong>Inside-door storage:</strong> slim racks on door backs for lids, wraps and cleaning items.</li>
<li><strong>Open shelf, sparingly:</strong> one open shelf for everyday items can lighten the look, but open storage shows dust and grease.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Keep it looking bigger</h2>
<ul>
<li>Lighter door colours reflect more light; keep surfaces and handles simple.</li>
<li>Under-cabinet lighting brightens the worktop and makes the room feel less cramped.</li>
<li>Choose finishes that wipe clean easily; see our <a href="/tips-for-cleaning-kitchen-cabinets/">cleaning guide</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Plan storage by what you use</h2>
<p>Group items by where you use them: pots near the hob, plates near the dishwasher or drying area, food near the prep surface. Then fit drawers, shelves and pull-outs to those groups. For more ideas on arranging what you already own, read <a href="/how-to-organize-kitchen-cabinets/">how to organize kitchen cabinets</a>.</p>

